<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad I of Granada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1195–1273</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Ibn al-Ahmar, Muhammad ibn Yusuf ibn Nasr</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, known as Ibn al-Ahmar, was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasrid-dynasty">Nasrid dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Granada. A minor noble from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjona">Arjona</a>, he proclaimed himself ruler in 1232 amid the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> power and made Granada his capital in 1238. He survived by becoming a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a> in 1246 and began building the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-i-of-granada.jpg" alt="Muhammad I of Granada" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Alfonso X of Castile, 13th century · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhammad_I_Granada_cropped_CSM_185_(187)_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
